pcm: hoist calc_tv's per-tick invariants out of the voice loop

calc_tv() runs 97 times per PCM tick -- three per voice slot plus one for the
pseudo-slot 31 chorus/reverb envelope -- and at 31% of everything pcm.cpp
retires it is the single most expensive thing in the file. Two of the things it
recomputes on every one of those calls do not vary per slot:

  * `addlow`, a four-bit reversal of four bits tapped out of pcm.tv_counter,
    selected by `type & 4` and then `type & 3`, and
  * the counter half of the `write` enable, from the same counter.

pcm.tv_counter is written exactly once per tick, immediately before the chorus/
reverb stage, and pcm.nfs is written once per tick at the very end, so within a
tick there are only five distinct (addlow, write) pairs. Compute all five once
per tick into a five-entry table and index it.

`type` itself, and which of the five taps it selects, are a pure function of the
8-bit `speed` field plus pcm.nfs -- and nfs only ever clears one bit of it. That
is a 256-byte compile-time table, built by the same expressions it replaces.

This is the third lookup table tried in this core and the first that is faster.
patches/README.md records why the PCM_ReadROM one lost: it replaced perfectly
predictable branches with a dependent load. This one replaces about ten
straight-line ALU operations with one load out of a 256-byte table that every
tick touches 97 times, so it is never anywhere but L1.

Measured on the ROM-free voice-loop harness, all 32 voices keyed on:
35098 -> 32458 Ir per tick (-7.5%). Splitting the two halves: the per-tick tap
table is worth 1748 Ir/tick and the `type` table a further 892.

